            "In the orchard I opened a new flower every morning, and each one looked for you. Today one finally found you.",
            "Mm, hello. The days went by in a drowse under the apple trees, and you kept reading the whole time. I could tell, somehow. I always can.",
            "You came for me, after all those days. I lived each one as the only day, and you were in every one of them. Let us go home.",
            "{name} wakes! My seeds wake when the ground is turned over, and I woke when your last page was. Hello, dreamer.",
        },
    },
    beats = {
        { id = "asleep", at = 15, title = "Asleep",
            text = "Before I ever saw you, I slept. Down in the dark soil, a seed no bigger than a speck, for longer than I can count. I dreamt the whole time. Mostly of red, and of a sky I had never seen." },
        { id = "plough", at = 40, title = "The plough",
            text = "One day the ground rolled over. Earth tumbled, light poured in, and I woke all at once, the way you wake from a dream of falling. I had no idea how long I had slept. Years. Many years, I think." },
        { id = "one_day", at = 80, title = "One day",
            text = "My first flower opened at dawn. By the next evening her petals lay on the ground, and I was frightened. Then another bud opened, and another. I learned that brief and gone are two different things." },
        { id = "pepper_pot", at = 140, title = "The pepper pot",
            text = "When summer ended I had a seed head like a little pepper pot, with holes round the top. The wind shook me and out went hundreds of seeds. I wondered where each would sleep, and for how long." },
        { id = "voice", at = 220, title = "The voice below",
            text = "In my long sleep there was a sound that came down through the soil. A voice, humming or reading, I could never tell which. I never saw whose. Some nights I still hear that voice, very faintly." },
        { id = "paper", at = 320, title = "The paper poppy",
            text = "Once a child stood in our field wearing a poppy made of paper, a red copy of me. People wear us to remember, her grandmother said, softly. I had not known we were for that. I was glad we were." },
        { id = "pressed", at = 450, title = "The pressed flower",
            text = "That same child picked one of my flowers and pressed the flower flat inside a heavy book. I never saw her again. Somewhere there is a book with a little red flower in the pages, still keeping a place." },
        { id = "second_sleep", at = 600, title = "The second sleep",
            text = "The field went back to grass, and grass never turns the soil. My seeds slipped under, and so did I. A second long sleep. I dreamt of the child, the paper flower, and that voice." },
        { id = "spade", at = 800, title = "The spade",
            text = "Then a spade bit into the earth above me and turned the ground, and there was light again. This garden. I never saw whose hands held the spade. I have my suspicions." },
        { id = "murmur", at = 1050, title = "The murmur",
            text = "When you read, now and then you murmur a line under your breath. The first time, I went very still. The sound was so like the voice in my long sleep. Maybe all readers sound alike from below. Maybe not." },
        { id = "awake", at = 1400, title = "Awake so long",
            text = "{total} pages together. I was made to wake for a day or two and sleep for years. With you I have been awake longer than any poppy I ever dreamt of. I think the pages keep me up." },
        { id = "turned", at = 1800, title = "Turned ground, turned pages",
            text = "I know now what wakes me: turned earth, and turned pages. When winter comes and I sleep again, read near the soil. I will hear you, the way I once heard that voice. And every summer the first red thing you see will be me, waking up to you." },
    },
